# The arrow of `later` only calls `f`, and a call takes the arrow: `f` is only called, and the arrow of `maker` lies in the frame. But the call is `push`, which keeps the arrow, so the finished code makes it in the heap. In front of that the state byte of `f` is asked, and what bears the mark of a frame is moved: the arrow captures the copy.
# The arrows are called when the frames of their makers are long gone, behind a collection and a recursion that writes over the stack.
# f1 = later, f3 = maker, f2 = deep
# -- source: capture-that-may-lie-in-a-frame-is-brought-to-the-heap-where-its-closure-is-made-there.ts
